# Every operation that names a position in a process notation. One row per filed
# `pm:Operation/foreignId`: the notation, the id inside it, and the operation here that claims it.
#
# The third `pm:ForeignId`
#   The model carries exactly three references out of a document: an `asrt:Part`, an
#   `asrt:Elimination/between`, and this. The part has `composition/part_references.sqlc` and a
#   rule that verifies it resolves; the elimination has `eliminations/references.sqlc` and an
#   observation, because the schema licenses a `between` that lands nowhere. Two things of one
#   type owe the same apparatus, and an asymmetry between them is visible from `ls` without reading
#   a line of SQL.
#
# No rule may judge it
#   This tree grows by rules, and a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist.
#   `pm:ForeignId`'s own annotation refuses the check outright: a notation plus an id names a
#   position in one file that no authority publishes, so a receiver sent looking for `Task_17` in a
#   normative list finds nothing there and reads it as a defect in the list. There is nothing to
#   resolve against, so `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc`'s shape copied across would accuse every
#   filing. The answer is the same one `between` got: report, never assert.
#
# The other arm is a fact, not a silence
#   `notationPosition` is required and takes `pm:StatedForeignId`, so an operation that names no
#   position says why: `none` is in no notation and somebody looked, `unmeasured` is a notation
#   exists and nobody located this operation in it, `notApplicable` is this filing has no notation
#   at all. This relation is the stated arm; `queries/observations/17-notation-references.sqlc`
#   reports both, because the population is the finding and the restriction alone would hide it.
#
# The notation is a language, not a document
#   That is the difference from a part. A part names another filing and `filing_identity` resolves
#   it. This names the OMG BPMN model URI, and the id names a node in whichever BPMN document
#   travels beside the filing. So the pair identifies a position relative to a document nobody here
#   holds, which is exactly the property that makes the crossing cost the receiver nothing.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- pm:Operation/pm:notationPosition, the stated arm: a notation plus an id.
WITH
operations AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/operations.sqlc)
)
SELECT o.filing, o.label, o.foreign_notation, o.foreign_id
FROM ( :compose(entries/operations.sqlc) ) o
WHERE o.foreign_notation IS NOT NULL
